1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Ancient humans didn't exercise. They never went for a run to hit a step count. They never did sets of anything.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
They never blocked out 45 minutes three times a week for cardiovascular health.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:18,000
The concept of deliberate physical activity performed for the purpose of maintaining bodily fitness separate from

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:25,000
the activities of daily survival did not exist for the overwhelming majority of human evolutionary history. and they

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:33,000
were in dramatically better physical condition than most modern humans. The skeletal evidence is not subtle. Bone

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:41,000
density in pre-aggricultural and early agricultural populations consistently exceeds bone density in modern populations.

7
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Muscle attachment sites, the roughened areas on bone where muscles anchor, are more pronounced in ancient skeletal

8
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
remains, indicating larger, stronger muscles than modern equivalents.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Biomechanical analysis of ancient human leg bones shows locomot patterns consistent with sustained varied

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:11,000
physical activity across a lifetime producing skeletal adaptations that competitive modern athletes rarely achieve. These were not athletic outliers. These were ordinary people.

11
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:20,000
People whose skeletal remains show none of the special treatment associated with high status individuals who might have had access to unusually good nutrition

12
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:28,000
or unusually light labor. These were everyday ancient humans. And their bones tell a story about physical capacity

13
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:35,000
that makes the average modern gym goer look sedentary by comparison. How how does a species that invented neither the

14
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:42,000
gym nor the jogging shoe maintain physical fitness superior to populations that have access to both? The answer is

15
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:50,000
a complete reframing of what exercise actually is, what it's for, and why the modern version of it is both necessary

16
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
and in a specific biological sense a poor substitute for what it replaced.

17
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Let's go through it properly. Start with the distinction that underlies the entire question because most people conflate two things that are

18
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:12,000
biologically quite different. Exercise in the modern sense is deliberate physical activity performed for fitness purposes. You do it specifically to get

19
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:20,000
or stay fit. It is separate from the rest of your life. It has a beginning and an end. It is a scheduled activity with a specific goal. The rest of your

20
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:27,000
day, the sitting, the driving, the desk work, the couch is not exercise. It is everything else. Physical activity in

21
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:36,000
the evolutionary sense is simply what you do with your body during the course of daily life. It includes walking to where you need to go, carrying what you

22
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:43,000
need to carry, performing the physical work that obtaining and preparing food requires, moving through varied terrain,

23
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:50,000
lifting, pulling, crouching, climbing, and all the other physical demands that a life without mechanical assistance

24
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:58,000
imposes. Ancient humans had essentially no modern exercise. They had essentially unlimited physical activity and the

25
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:05,000
biological consequences of each are not equivalent even when the total caloric expenditure is similar. This distinction

26
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:14,000
matters because the human body evolved in an environment of continuous physical activity, not an environment of sedentary life punctuated by exercise

27
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:23,000
sessions. the specific adaptations that make the human body function well. The cardiovascular system, the muscular scalidal system, the metabolic system,

28
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:31,000
the hormonal systems that regulate all of these were calibrated to continuous varied moderate physical activity as the

29
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:38,000
baseline condition, not as the periodic exception. Now, let's talk about what ancient physical activity actually

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:47,000
looked like because the picture is more interesting and more varied than the simple image of ancient humans doing a lot of walking. The Hadza people of

31
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Tanzania are one of the most extensively studied modern forager populations and represent the closest living analog to the foraging lifestyle that

32
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:04,000
characterized most of human evolutionary history. Men in Hadza communities walk approximately 15 to 19 km per day during

33
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:11,000
foraging activity. Women walk approximately 9 to 12 km per day. These figures are not exceptional. They

34
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:18,000
represent the ordinary baseline of daily foraging activity for a population whose lifestyle closely tracks ancestral human

35
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:26,000
foraging patterns. But the walking is not the whole story. Foraging walking is not the flat paved rhythmically

36
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:34,000
consistent walking of a suburban sidewalk. It is walking over varied terrain, slopes, loose substrate, dense

37
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:42,000
vegetation that recruits muscle groups and demands balance and proprioceptive engagement that flat surface walking

38
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:50,000
does not. It involves frequent changes of pace, direction, and elevation. It includes squatting to examine ground

39
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:57,000
level plants and tracks, climbing to access elevated resources, crouching to pass through dense vegetation, reaching

40
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:06,000
overhead to gather fruit or nuts. The variety of movement patterns is the key feature. The human muscularkeeletal system evolved in an environment where

41
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:15,000
every day included a wide range of movement types. Not repetitive single plane movements performed at fixed resistance, which is essentially what

42
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:22,000
modern exercise provides, but the full three-dimensional constantly varying movement vocabulary that navigating an

43
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:32,000
outdoor environment across varied terrain requires. Carrying is another major component of ancient physical activity that is almost entirely absent from modern life.

44
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:40,000
Hunter gatherer populations carry significant loads regularly. Gathered food, water containers, small children,

45
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:47,000
tools, materials for construction and repair. Load carrying is not the same movement pattern as unloaded walking. It

46
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:57,000
recruits the postural muscles of the trunk, the muscles of the shoulder girdle, and the stabilizing muscles of the hip and knee in ways that unloaded

47
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:05,000
movement does not. The skeletal evidence of load carrying in ancient populations, the specific patterns of bone hypertrophy associated with sustained

48
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:14,000
loadbearing is clearly visible in the archaeological record. Now let's talk about the hormonal and metabolic dimension because this is where the

49
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:22,000
difference between ancient physical activity and modern exercise becomes most consequential and most counterintuitive.

50
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Exercise sessions, particularly highintensity exercise sessions, produce acute physiological stress responses.

51
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Heart rate elevates dramatically.

52
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:37,000
Cortisol and adrenaline release. The sympathetic nervous system activates.

53
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:45,000
These are appropriate responses to acute physical stress and they are followed in a healthy physiology by recovery by a

54
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:52,000
return to baseline that includes anabolic processes like muscle protein synthesis, bone remodeling and

55
00:06:52,000 --> 00:07:00,000
cardiovascular adaptation. This acute stress recovery cycle is the mechanism by which exercise produces fitness adaptations.

56
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:07,000
The stress of exercise signals to the body that increased capacity is required and the body responds during recovery by

57
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:14,000
increasing capacity. This is why exercise works. This is also why exercise is a fundamentally different

58
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:21,000
physiological stimulus from the chronic moderate physical activity that characterized ancient human life.

59
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:30,000
Moderate continuous physical activity, the walking, carrying, foraging, and manual work of a huntergatherer lifestyle does not produce the same

60
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:37,000
acute stress response that a sprint or a heavy lifting session produces. It produces a different and in some

61
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:46,000
respects more profound physiological signal. the signal that the body is being used continuously at moderate intensity across a wide range of

62
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:54,000
movement patterns and that all of its systems need to be maintained at a level adequate for this continuous use. The

63
00:07:54,000 --> 00:08:03,000
chronic low to moderate physical activity signal that ancient humans physiology was receiving continuously is actually the primary stimulus for many

64
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:12,000
of the most important health-maintaining physiological processes. It is the primary stimulus for mitochondrial biogenesis. The production of new

65
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:20,000
mitochondria in muscle cells that is the foundation of metabolic health and insulin sensitivity. It is the primary stimulus for the maintenance of

66
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:28,000
cardiovascular baseline health. Resting heart rate, stroke volume, arterial flexibility

67
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:36,000
that acute exercise sessions only temporarily elevate. It is the primary signal for the maintenance of bone density across all regions of the

68
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:44,000
skeleton. Not just the regions stressed by specific exercise movements. Modern people who exercise, even those who

69
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:52,000
exercise regularly, typically spend the vast majority of their waking hours sedentary. The 2 hours a week of vigorous exercise that most fitness

70
00:08:52,000 --> 00:09:02,000
guidelines recommend is attempting to provide a substitute for what was previously a continuous physiological background signal. It does not fully

71
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:10,000
substitute. The physiological research consistently finds that the health outcomes of physically active populations, those who achieve activity

72
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:19,000
through their daily lives rather than through structured exercise, are better than the health outcomes of populations that exercise equivalently but spend the

73
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:28,000
majority of their remaining time sedentary. This is not an argument against exercise. Exercise provides acute fitness adaptations that chronic

74
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:36,000
moderate activity alone does not produce. the strength gains from resistance training, the cardiovascular capacity gains from highintensity

75
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:44,000
aerobic training, the specific metabolic adaptations from highintensity interval exercise. These are real and valuable,

76
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:53,000
but they are not replacements for the continuous baseline physical activity that the body evolved to receive and that it is not receiving in a modern

77
00:09:53,000 --> 00:10:01,000
sedentary context. By the way, if you want to go even deeper into the secret lives of ancient humans, I just launched

78
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:10,000
a full course called The Secret Lives of Ancient Humans. 10 modules, 3 plus hours of content covering everything we never

79
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:21,000
talk about on YouTube. What they feared, how they communicated, what hunted them, and much more. Plus, an exclusive PDF guide included. Link in the description.

80
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:28,000
See you inside. Now, let's talk about childhood specifically because the physical activity patterns of childhood are particularly important for

81
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:36,000
understanding why ancient humans didn't need exercise [snorts] and why modern humans increasingly do. Children in traditional societies and in modern

82
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:44,000
societies before the 20th century spend most of their waking hours in outdoor physical activity. Not organized sport,

83
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:51,000
not directed exercise, but the spontaneous physical play that children universally engage in when given unstructured time in physical

84
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:58,000
environments. Running, climbing, jumping, rolling, wrestling, carrying,

85
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:07,000
throwing. The movement vocabulary of free outdoor play is extraordinarily rich and developmentally important in ways that are only beginning to be

86
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:16,000
appreciated by developmental researchers. This childhood physical activity is not trivial from a long-term health perspective. The bone density

87
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:24,000
that carries into adulthood is substantially determined by the physical loading that bone receives during childhood and adolescence. Bone is most

88
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:33,000
responsive to loading stimulus during growth and the loading patterns of active childhood produce bone density and bone geometry that passive childhood

89
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:42,000
cannot replicate later. The cardiovascular baseline established during active childhood persists into adult life in ways that make adult

90
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:49,000
cardiovascular health partly a function of childhood activity patterns. The neuromuscular coordination patterns developed through varied childhood

91
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:56,000
physical play establish movement competency that affects injury risk and physical capability throughout adult

92
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:05,000
life. Ancient children were physically active throughout childhood not because anyone organized their activity, but because children in environments without

93
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:12,000
screens, without cars, without the thousand other substitutes for physical engagement that modern childhood offers

94
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:21,000
defaulted to movement as the primary activity of unoccupied time. Physical development consequences of this default were profound and longlasting. Modern

95
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:29,000
children are physically inactive at historically unprecedented levels. The epidemic of childhood sedentary behavior, the replacement of outdoor

96
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:38,000
physical play with screenbased indoor activity is producing skeletal, cardiovascular, and metabolic profiles in children that would have been

97
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:42,000
exceptional in ancient populations, but are increasingly normal in modern ones.

98
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:50,000
The long-term health consequences of growing up physically inactive are just beginning to be visible in the health outcomes of the cohorts who experienced

99
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:57,000
this most completely. Now, let's talk about something that almost never appears in fitness discussions, but that the research on ancient physical

100
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:04,000
activity makes surprisingly clear. The role of sleep and rest in making the ancient physical activity model work.

101
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:13,000
Ancient humans didn't just move more than modern humans. They also rested differently. The rest that followed a day of sustained moderate physical

102
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:20,000
activity was qualitatively different from the rest that follows a day of sustained sedentary behavior punctuated by an exercise session. The

103
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:28,000
physiological state of the body entering sleep after a day of continuous moderate activity is different from the physiological state of a body entering

104
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:36,000
sleep after a day of sitting. Deep sleep, slowwave sleep, the stage most associated with physical restoration and

105
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:46,000
growth hormone secretion, is more abundant and more restorative following days of sustained physical activity than following days of sedentary behavior.

106
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:53,000
Growth hormone secreted during deep sleep drives the anabolic processes, muscle protein synthesis, bone

107
00:13:53,000 --> 00:14:01,000
remodeling, tissue repair that maintain physical capacity in active individuals. The relationship is birectional.

108
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:10,000
Activity promotes better sleep and better sleep promotes better physical restoration and adaptation from activity. The modern pattern, sedentary

109
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:18,000
days punctuated by exercise sessions followed by sleep that is often inadequate in duration and disrupted in quality by artificial light and

110
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:26,000
irregular schedules breaks both sides of this relationship. The exercise session provides acute anabolic stimulus. The

111
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:33,000
inadequate sleep limits the anabolic response to that stimulus. The sedentary day limits the chronic baseline signal that would otherwise be driving

112
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:41,000
continuous maintenance of physical systems. The result is a physiological environment that is significantly less effective at maintaining physical

113
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:49,000
capacity than the ancient environment of continuous moderate activity followed by quality restorative sleep. Now let's talk about the cognitive dimension

114
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:57,000
because the connection between physical activity and brain function in ancient humans reveals another aspect of why the modern exercise sedentary split produces

115
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:06,000
inferior outcomes to the ancient continuous activity model. Physical activity is not just a body level phenomenon. It is a brain level

116
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:14,000
phenomenon with effects that are as significant for cognitive function as they are for physical function. The relationship between physical activity

117
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:23,000
and brain health is mediated partly through brain derived neurotrophic factor BDNF a protein that promotes the growth and

118
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:30,000
maintenance of neurons and that is released during sustained moderate physical activity. BDNF is sometimes

119
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:39,000
called miracle growth for the brain by researchers which is both reductive and accurate. It promotes neurogenesis in the hippocampus, the brain region most

120
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:46,000
critical for learning and memory and its levels declined dramatically in sedentary individuals. Ancient humans

121
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:51,000
were continuously releasing BDNF throughout their physically active days.

122
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:57,000
Their brains were receiving continuous neurotrphic support that maintained and enhanced the cognitive functions.

123
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:05,000
spatial navigation, working memory, attention that foraging and hunting demanded, and that the physical activity

124
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:10,000
simultaneously supported. The connection was circular and mutually reinforcing.

125
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:20,000
The physical activity required intelligence to perform well, and it simultaneously provided the neurochemical support that maintained the intelligence required to perform it.

126
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:28,000
Modern sedentary life breaks this relationship. Sitting at a desk does not drive BDNF release. The cognitive

127
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:35,000
consequences of chronic sedentary behavior, the reduced hippocample volume, the accelerated cognitive aging,

128
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:42,000
the increased dementia risk are increasingly documented in the research literature and are consistent with what

129
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:50,000
you would predict from a brain that is not receiving the continuous neurotrophic support that evolved to be its normal maintenance signal. Now let's

130
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:57,000
talk about posture and positional variety because this is a dimension of ancient physical activity that is almost

131
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:04,000
never discussed in fitness contexts and that is physiologically extremely important. Modern sedentary life

132
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:11,000
involves spending most of the day in approximately the same position seated with the hips flexed at approximately

133
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:20,000
90° the spine in a relatively fixed flexed position. The shoulder girdle protracted, the neck in forward flexion.

134
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:28,000
This is not the position the human muscular sceadal system was designed for as a sustained resting posture. It is a position that sustained for hours daily

135
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:36,000
over years produces predictable patterns of muscular dysfunction. hip flexor shortening, posterior chain weakening,

136
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:43,000
thoracic kyphosis, cervical dysfunction that are the direct cause of the muscular scaladal pain that is one of

137
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:52,000
the most common medical complaints in developed countries. Ancient humans spent their resting time in a variety of postures, squatting, sitting on the

138
00:17:52,000 --> 00:18:01,000
ground with legs extended or crossed, kneeling, reclining in various positions. Research on traditional populations that maintain ground sitting

139
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:10,000
habits shows much lower rates of hip flexor shortening, posterior chain dysfunction, and the spinal problems associated with sustained chair sitting

140
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:19,000
than populations that have adopted chair-based sedentary postures. The squat is particularly important. The full deep squat, resting with the hips

141
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:26,000
below the knees, heels on the ground, requires hip, ankle, and thoracic mobility that most modern adults have

142
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:34,000
lost. It is the natural human resting posture documented across forager and traditional agricultural populations

143
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:42,000
globally. And it maintains the range of motion in these joints that simply sitting in a chair does not. The loss of deep squat mobility in modern

144
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:49,000
populations is partly a consequence of spending childhood and adulthood in chairs rather than on the ground. And it has downstream consequences for movement

145
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:58,000
quality and injury risk throughout the body. Ancient humans didn't need to do mobility work or stretching routines because they spent their days in the

146
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:05,000
postures and movement patterns that maintained mobility naturally. The modern requirement for dedicated mobility work is the requirement to

147
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:09,000
recover a range of motion that the lifestyle has progressively removed.

148
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:17,000
Now, let's talk about what the modern exercise solution actually is and what it isn't. Because the picture that the ancient physical activity evidence

149
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:25,000
paints has specific implications for how modern people should think about fitness. The modern exercise solution dedicated sessions of structured

150
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:34,000
physical activity performed in addition to an otherwise sedentary life is a compensatory adaptation. It is an attempt to provide the physiological

151
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:42,000
signals that the body evolved to receive continuously in compressed periodic doses. It works partially and

152
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:49,000
imperfectly. The acute adaptations that exercise produces, improved cardiovascular capacity, increased

153
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:56,000
muscle strength, improved body composition are real and valuable, but they do not fully substitute for the

154
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:04,000
chronic background activity signal that the body evolved to require. The research on non-ex exercise physical activity, the walking, standing,

155
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:12,000
fidgeting, carrying, and other incidental physical activity that happens outside of formal exercise sessions, consistently finds that this

156
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:20,000
background activity is as important for metabolic health outcomes as formal exercise. And for some outcomes, more important, people who achieve high

157
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:27,000
levels of non-ex exercise physical activity show better insulin sensitivity, better lipid profiles, and better cardiovascular markers than

158
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:32,000
people who exercise equally but spend more of their remaining time sedentary.

159
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:39,000
This finding has a practical implication that is both obvious and consistently ignored in modern fitness culture. The

160
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:48,000
background activity matters as much as the exercise session. Sitting for 8 hours and exercising for 1 hour is physiologically worse than walking

161
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:56,000
throughout the day and not exercising at all. The hour of exercise does not compensate for the 8 hours of sitting because the signals they provide to the

162
00:20:56,000 --> 00:21:04,000
body are different in kind, not just in quantity. And this is ultimately what the question in the title is really about. Ancient humans didn't need

163
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:11,000
exercise because their lives were exercise. Not as a periodic intense stimulus, but as a continuous moderate

164
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:19,000
background that the body received every waking hour. The foraging, the carrying, the squatting, the walking over varied

165
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:27,000
terrain, the manual work of food processing and camp maintenance. These activities were not fitness activities.

166
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:35,000
They were survival activities but their physiological effects were the maintenance of the physical capacity that the survival required and the

167
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:43,000
bodies they produced were the product of that maintenance. Modern humans need exercise because modern life has removed the continuous moderate physical

168
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:50,000
activity that the body evolved to receive. Replacing it with sustained sedentary behavior that the body was

169
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:59,000
never designed for. The exercise session is the attempt to compensate for that removal. It is better than nothing. It is not as good as the thing it replaced.

170
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:08,000
The ancient human was not a fitness enthusiast. They were just a person living a life that required their body to work. And their skeleton 10,000 years

171
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:11,000
later is still impressing archaeologists.
